  "text": "Many investigators have looked at separating isotopes by using ion‑exchange columns or by moving ions with an electric field, but keeping these processes running continuously still presents problems. A novel device was built that combines ion‑exchange with electric‑field‑driven migration, allowing the feed to be processed without interruption. To test how well it works, experiments were performed on the isotopes of a light alkali metal. When the system reached a steady state, the enrichment factors measured for two different chemical forms of the metal were about 1.007 and 1.005, respectively. These figures are higher than those obtained with earlier ion‑exchange media (around 1.001–1.004) and lower than the best values reported for pure electric‑field migration through a specialized membrane (about 1.008)."
